<h2>How it is built</h2>
<pre><code>Pointer events (browser)
  -> batched once per animation frame
  -> BinkStrokeBuilder        spacing filter, streamline smoothing, pressure
  -> BinkStroke + BinkBrush   immutable vector model
  -> BinkDrawingOperation     the unit of undo, diffing and collaboration
  -> BinkDocument             layers of elements
  -> IBinkRenderContext       canvas commands, SVG, or the built-in rasterizer</code></pre>

<p>
    One rendering path serves the screen, SVG export and PNG export, so the exported file always matches what
    was drawn. Pointer input never triggers a Blazor render: samples arrive once per frame, the active tool
    processes them, and only the affected canvas layer is redrawn.
</p>

<div class="note">
    In WebAssembly the command buffer reaches JavaScript as a view over managed memory, so a redraw costs one
    interop call and no serialization. In Blazor Server the same engine runs on the server and a lightweight
    trail is drawn locally while each batch is in flight, so the pen still feels attached to the hand.
</div>

<h2>Get started</h2>
<pre><code>dotnet add package Bit.Bink</code></pre>
<pre><code>@@using Bit.Bink

&lt;BinkCanvas Height="400px" /&gt;</code></pre>
<p>
    The stylesheet is added by the package's JavaScript initializer, so there is nothing to put in the host page.
    The source code, the documentation and the code of this demo are on
    <a href="https://github.com/bitfoundation/bitplatform/tree/develop/src/Bink" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
</p>

@code {
    private BinkCanvas? _canvas;
    private readonly BinkBrush _brush = new BinkPenBrush { Width = 4f, Color = BinkColor.Parse("#111827") };

    private void Undo() => _canvas?.Undo();

    private void Clear() => _canvas?.Clear();
}
